<!--
  checker/companion-audit.dtd : the contract of the Scratchpad Companion.

  The companion is a second Claude session, run in the FOREGROUND by
  checker/companion-audit.sh after each build phase: `claude -p` with the
  audit model, stdin closed, a turn ceiling, a wall-clock ceiling, output
  teed to the scratchpad, exit code read through PIPESTATUS. It reads the
  phase's diff and the tree and answers in the grammar below. It never
  edits, never spawns, never backgrounds, never runs a command that reads
  stdin, and never runs a command without its own ceiling: the anti-stall
  laws are part of the contract, not advice.

  This file lives under checker/ and not dtd/ on purpose: it is an
  instrument of the checker, not a subset any command includes, so the
  contract audit (every dtd/ declaration used by src/) does not apply.
-->

<!ELEMENT companion_audit (scope, finding*, verdict, next)>
<!ATTLIST companion_audit phase CDATA #REQUIRED range CDATA #REQUIRED model CDATA #REQUIRED>
<!ELEMENT scope (#PCDATA)>
<!ELEMENT finding (#PCDATA)>
<!ATTLIST finding
          file       CDATA #REQUIRED
          line       CDATA #REQUIRED
          severity   (high|medium|low) #REQUIRED
          confidence (measured|reasoned|guessed) #REQUIRED>
<!-- The verdict is prose; the decision travels as the LAST line of the
     answer, one of the two entities below, which the scorer reads. No
     attribute: nothing read one, so none is declared. -->
<!ELEMENT verdict (#PCDATA)>
<!ELEMENT next (#PCDATA)>

<!ENTITY COMPANION.verdict.pass "COMPANION VERDICT: pass">
<!ENTITY COMPANION.verdict.fail "COMPANION VERDICT: fail">

<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.1 "The companion reads and runs; it never writes, edits, deletes, commits, spawns an agent or backgrounds a process.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.2 "Every command the companion runs carries its own timeout ceiling and has stdin closed; a command that reads stdin is never run.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.3 "A finding names a file and a line it read, its severity and its confidence; measured requires a thing that was run or read in this audit.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.4 "The answer ends with exactly one verdict line, COMPANION.verdict.pass or COMPANION.verdict.fail, and fail requires at least one high finding.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.5 "A ceiling that fires (exit 124) is a result: the phase is recorded as unaudited, never as passed.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.6 "The scope opens with one line carrying the three attributes of companion_audit, phase= range= model=, and the runner refuses an answer whose line does not match the run it launched.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.7 "The runner stamps the record it writes with the run that wrote it and scores no record carrying another stamp; a run that produced no answer is unaudited, never scored on an older record.">
<!ENTITY LAW.COMPANION.8 "The four elements appear in the answer as their headings in declared order, Scope, Findings, Verdict, Next; a pass without them is not a pass.">

<!-- Which instrument holds which law. 1 and 2: the runner's tool allow-list
     (Read, Grep, Glob, and one Bash form, the wrapper
     checker/companion-run.sh, which runs an engine of this repository or a
     reading git verb under the portable ceiling with stdin closed and
     refuses everything else; since 9.0.0, after a prefix grant admitted
     node -e; M17 refuses a copy granting Write, M23 a copy granting the old
     prefix, M24 the wrapper's own refusals) and the tree compared before and
     after the session (M25 plants a changed tree); a write reached some
     other way is the companion's own breach, and it discloses it in the
     scope. 3, 4 and 6: the scorer in checker/companion-audit.sh (a finding
     element carries file, line, severity and confidence in its opening
     tag, M16; exactly one verdict line, M18 with two and M19 with none; a
     fail needs one high finding, counted in the opening tag only, M9 to
     M11, M14 and M15; the scope line, M12 and M13). 5: the runner's exit
     124 branch, M26 fires it with a claude that sleeps past a one-second
     ceiling and reads UNAUDITED. 7: the stamp, written by the runner and required by the
     scorer (M20 another run's stamp, M22 a stamped record scored with no
     stamp). 8: the four headings in declared order (M12 for a pass that
     carries them, M21 for the order). -->
